    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d650">

                <pb facs="0835" n="825" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg825"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/4–2847</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Director of the Office of Far Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Vincent</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1947-05-02">May 2, 1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Nanking’s message is indicative of what we may expect from the Chinese Government
                    in the near future in the way of a request for military and economic
                    assistance.</p>
                <p>[Here follows summary of telegram No. 916, April 28, midnight, from the
                    Ambassador in China, printed <hi rendition="#italic">supra</hi>].</p>
                <p>Following are our recommendations: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Financial assistance</hi>. We await the
                            presentation of a formal request, which is anticipated shortly.</item>
                        <pb facs="0836" n="826" xml:id="pg826"/>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Ammunition</hi> and 3. <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >8</hi>⅓ <hi rendition="#italic">group program</hi>. Inasmuch as the
                            problem of military assistance to China is now under review by Joint
                            Chiefs of Staff, we should await their recommendations.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">American military adviser</hi>. General
                                Lucas<anchor n="95" xml:id="d650-f1"/> is already in China and we
                            perceive no reason for sending an additional adviser.<anchor n="96"
                                xml:id="d650-f2"/>
                        </item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>J[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ohn</hi>] C. V[<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >incent</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d650-f1" n="95" xml:id="d650-f1-content">
                <p>Maj. Gen. John P. Lucas, Chief, American Army Advisory Group in China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d650-f2" n="96" xml:id="d650-f2-content">
                <p>An attached memorandum of May 6 indicated agreement by the Secretary of State
                    with these recommendations.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
